Lab: Secure a Small Wireless Network
In this scenario, I installed a new router in my home office and connected my desktop computer (Home-PC) to the router with an Ethernet cable. The next step was to set up the wireless network so I could connect my laptop (Home-Laptop) over Wi-Fi. To configure the wireless access point, I used Chrome on Home-PC.

Address: 192.168.0.254
Username: admin
Password: password

If this were a real network, I would change those right away, but that's not part of this lab.

Required Actions
 Give the wireless network an SSID
 Give the wireless network WPA2 Personal encryption
 Give the wireless network a password
 Connect the laptop to the wireless network

Here’s a breakdown of the steps:

Access the router settings:
a. Click the screen of Home-PC to access the operating system.
b. From the taskbar, I opened Chrome.
c. In the URL field, I typed 192.168.0.254 and pressed Enter.
d. In the Username field, I entered admin.
e. In the Password field, I entered password.
f. I selected OK.

Give the network an SSID (network name):
The SSID can be changed on the Basic Settings subtab of the Wireless tab.
I added the SSID Pretty Fly Wi-Fi to the 2.4GHz interface.
Before leaving this page, I scrolled to the bottom and clicked Apply Settings to save my work.

Add encryption and a password to the wireless network:
The encryption and password were configured on the Wireless Security subtab of the Wireless tab.

I used WPA2-PSK encryption.
I set H@rd2Guess as the shared key (password).
Before leaving this page, I scrolled to the bottom and clicked Apply to save my work.

Return to the Computer Desk and connect the laptop to the wireless network I just configured.

This lab helped me practice basic wireless router configuration and security practices essential for home and small office networks.
